# PCI ID fragment for the rocm-ernic emulated NIC (0x1dd8:0x100a).
#
# Usually you do not need this. The device server presents a real,
# vendor-assigned pair, and current hwdata already lists 1dd8:100a --
# as "DSC Serial Port Controller", which is what lspci will call the
# emulated NIC. Misleading, but named.
#
# The fragment is here for a guest whose pci.ids predates that entry,
# where lspci shows a bare device id instead. Merge the device line
# under the existing "1dd8" vendor block in your system pci.ids;
# typical paths are /usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids and
# /usr/share/misc/pci.ids.
#
# Two things will bite you if you do it by hand:
#
#  - pci.ids is parsed sequentially, so the device line must sit
#    inside the vendor block. Appending it to the end of the file
#    puts it under the class section, where it never matches.
#  - pciutils rejects a file containing a duplicate device id
#    outright: lspci then exits 1 and resolves no names for any
#    device on the bus, not just this one. So check the vendor block
#    does not already list 100a before adding it.
#
# ernic_guest_setup does both checks for you (tasks/pci_ids.yml);
# keep ernic_guest_pciids_entry in its defaults in step with the
# line below. Note that update-pciids overwrites the system pci.ids
# and discards the edit; re-run the role after each update.
#
1dd8  AMD Pensando Systems
	100a  ROCm Emulated RDMA NIC (ionic)
